  "name": "9 V power · DC jack and battery",
  "version": {
    "major": 1,
    "minor": 3
  },
  "description": "How a pedal takes a 9 V supply or a battery. The DC jack (centre negative) puts the supply on V+, through a reverse-polarity diode and the 9 V input filter to V+F (the filter's values from the ProCo Rat, ElectroSmash; the 1N5817 as on Aion FX's boards). The battery's + goes to the jack's battery lug, which touches the sleeve only with no plug in, so a supply cuts the battery off; its - goes to ground: in a real pedal that wire goes to the input jack's ring, so the battery is on only with a guitar cable plugged in (the simulation always has a cable in). Knobs: Power picks the DC jack or the battery; the battery's form sets how drained it is.",
  "tags": [
    "power",
    "battery",
    "filter"
  ],
